# tools/ales_stereo_normalize_witness.rish — stereo_normalize_peak: peak normalization carried into stereo with ONE SHARED GAIN, the linked normalize that preserves the stereo image (ALES129).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES129.
# Design read: active-designing/20260815-025919_fill-ales129-lotus-stereo-normalize.md
# Stands on: lotus/normalize.rye (ALES103 — the mono peak normalizer, its NormalizeError reused by name), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip, two mono Clips left/right, and its balance
# invariant), lotus/meter.rye (ALES13 — the peak/RMS meter, its measure_stereo reads both channels read-only and peak reads the loudest of each), lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2 — the Clip and its
# saturating gain). A Lotus master is a StereoClip whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. This rung opens the stereo AMPLITUDE class on the one member where the
# trivial per-channel lift would be a bug: normalize each channel to the target independently and the quieter channel's peak is dragged up to the target too, collapsing the inter-channel level
# difference that IS the stereo image. The honest lift is linked: measure both channels' peaks, take the larger (the master peak), scale BOTH channels by the SAME fraction target/master_peak.
#
# lotus/stereo_normalize.rye refuses a zero or over-ceiling target (BadTarget) before any read, measures both channels through ALES13, leaves a both-silent master exactly as it is (no divide by
# zero), else scales both channels by target/master_peak through ALES2's gain. The crux is five laws. THE LINKED-SCALE LAW: each channel equals ALES2's gain with the shared denominator byte for
# byte. THE MASTER-PEAK LAW: after normalize the master peak (max of both channel peaks) equals the target exactly, including the i16 minimum at the domain ceiling. THE IMAGE-PRESERVING LAW: a 2:1
# master to 16000 leaves left 16000 and right 8000, the ratio held, where independent normalize would flatten it. THE BALANCE / SILENCE LAW: the channels stay equal-length, a both-silent pair and
# an empty pair unchanged. THE IDEMPOTENCE LAW WITH REFUSALS: twice equals once, a zero and an over-ceiling target each refuse BadTarget untouched.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_stereo_normalize_witness.rish

say "ales-stereo-normalize: Language EN — stereo_normalize_peak scales a stereo master so its loudest sample anywhere, across both channels, lands exactly on a chosen target, while the two channels keep their relative level. A Lotus master is two Clips heard together, one per speaker, whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length so the two channels stay aligned in time. This rung opens the stereo amplitude class on the one member where the trivial per-channel lift would be a bug: normalize each channel to the target independently and the quieter channel's peak is dragged up to the target too, collapsing the inter-channel level difference that IS the stereo image — a vocal panned slightly left would drift to dead centre. The honest lift is linked: measure the peak of both channels through ALES13, take the larger (the master peak), and scale both channels by the same fraction target/master_peak through ALES2's gain, so the channel holding the master peak lands on the target and the other stays proportionally quieter. It is every DAW's Normalize (linked / stereo), a separate button from per-channel normalize for exactly this reason."
say "ales-stereo-normalize: Style Radiant — the rule is stated positively: refuse a zero or over-ceiling target before any read, measure both channels' peaks, leave a both-silent master exactly as it is (no divide by zero), else scale both channels by the one shared fraction target/master_peak. It reuses ALES103's NormalizeError by name and ALES2's gain for the arithmetic, inventing no new fault and no new primitive. The crux stands out as five laws: the linked-scale law (each channel equals ALES2's gain with the shared denominator byte for byte, so a master with left peak 8000 and right peak 4000 to 16000 is left times two and right times two, not right times four), the master-peak law (after normalize the master peak, the max of both channel peaks re-measured through ALES13, equals the target exactly whichever channel holds it, including the i16 minimum at the domain ceiling), the image-preserving law (a 2:1 master to 16000 leaves left 16000 and right 8000, the ratio held, where independent normalize would have driven both to 16000 and flattened the field), the balance / silence law (the channels stay equal-length, a both-silent pair and an empty pair unchanged whatever the target), and the idempotence law with refusals (normalize twice to one target equals once, and a zero target or a target past sample_max each refuse BadTarget with both channels untouched)."
say "ales-stereo-normalize: Lens TAME — two bounded i16 Clips (left, right) scaled in lockstep by one shared gain; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, the target is bound-checked before any read, and the both-silent path returns before the division so the denominator is always the measured non-zero master peak; measure_stereo reads both channels read-only through ALES13, peak reads the loudest of each, the max is taken as the master peak, and gain is run on each channel over the whole span [0, len) with the shared numerator target and shared denominator master_peak, so no BadRange and no BadGain can land and the forwarded EditError is honestly unreachable; the linked-scale law is proven against ALES2's own gain run with the shared denominator per channel, the master-peak law re-measures both channels and checks the max across four masters (loud channel left, loud channel right, the domain-ceiling floor, an alternating pair) at five targets, the image-preserving law proves a 2:1 field keeps its ratio and trips a guard if the right ever reaches the target, and the two BadTarget refusals leave both channels byte-for-byte untouched; siloed to lotus/, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/stereo_normalize"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/stereo_normalize.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-stereo-normalize: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/stereo_normalize.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-stereo-normalize: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-stereo-normalize: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-stereo-normalize' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-stereo-normalize: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-stereo-normalize line"
